<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Credit Suisse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/2023">2023</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Schweizerische Kreditanstalt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Credit Suisse was a Swiss bank founded in Zurich in 1856 by Alfred Escher, originally to finance railway building, and for long one of the country's two largest banks. After a series of losses and scandals, a loss of confidence in March 2023 led the Swiss authorities to arrange its emergency takeover by UBS, ending its existence as an independent bank.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/credit-suisse.jpg" alt="Credit Suisse" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland zh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Credit_Suisse_-_Paradeplatz_2011-08-01_16-35-48_ShiftN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Alfred Escher, called the spiritual father of the railway law of 1852 for his work against a state-run railway system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, founded the bank with the Allgemeine Deutsche Credit-Anstalt on 5 July 1856. Its first name was the Swiss Credit Institution (Schweizerische Kreditanstalt). Its main purpose was to provide domestic funding for railway projects and so avoid French banks that wanted to exert influence over the railways.</p><p>Escher aimed to start with three million shares and instead sold 218 million in three days. The bank opened on 16 July 1856 and was modelled on the Crédit Mobilier, a French railway-financing bank founded two years earlier, though with a more conservative policy of short-to-medium-term loans. In its first year 25% of its revenues came from the Swiss Northeastern Railway, which was being built by Bruno Hildebrand and Escher's company, Nordostbahn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The bank issued loans that helped create <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>'s electrical grid and the European rail system. In the 1900s it began to shift towards retail banking, responding to the rise of the middle class and to competition from the Swiss banks UBS and Julius Bär. In 1978 it partnered with First Boston to form Credit Suisse First Boston in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and bought a 44% stake in First Boston's American operations, and in 1988 it acquired a controlling share of First Boston itself. Between 1990 and 2000 it bought institutions such as the Winterthur Group, Swiss Volksbank and Bank Leu.</p><p>Credit Suisse came through the credit crisis better than many competitors and did not have to borrow from the government. In 2007 two of its traders pleaded guilty to mismarking securities positions to overvalue them by $3 billion and so raise their year-end bonuses. 6 billion in fines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a>.</p><p>On 15 March 2023 its share price fell nearly 25% after its largest investor, Saudi National Bank, said it could not provide more financial assistance. UBS took the bank over, and in July 2024 Credit Suisse (Schweiz) ceased to exist as a separate legal entity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Credit Suisse Group AG was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joint-stock-company">joint-stock company</a> registered in Zurich that operated as a holding company, with offices in the major financial centres. It offered investment banking, private banking and asset management, and it was known for strict bank-client confidentiality and banking secrecy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Credit Suisse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The global financial crisis of 2008 required a federal rescue of UBS and, under American and international pressure, Switzerland accepted the automatic exchange of tax information, ending traditional bank secrecy towards foreign authorities; in 2023 <b>Credit Suisse</b> was taken over by UBS with government support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
